$ oops explain
oops: oops explain needs a command to check. Try: oops explain rm -rf build
[exit 2]

$ oops explain rm -rf
Harmless. rm -rf with no path deletes nothing, so there is nothing to save.
[exit 0]

$ oops explain git reset --hard
Destructive. Before it runs, Oopsy saves:
  your branch position and uncommitted changes before git reset --hard in $WORK
Anything else it changes in $WORK is caught by the project mirror too.
[exit 0]

$ oops explain ls -la
Read only. ls -la only reads files, so Oopsy lets it run with no snapshot and no cost.
[exit 0]

$ oops lsit
oops: oops lsit is not a command. Did you mean oops list? Run oops --help to see them all.
[exit 2]

$ oops undoo 3
oops: oops undoo is not a command. Did you mean oops undo? Run oops --help to see them all.
[exit 2]

$ oops undo
oops: oops undo needs the id of a snapshot (oops list shows them). Try: oops undo 12
[exit 2]

$ oops undo 99
oops: there is no snapshot 99. oops list shows the ones you have (they are kept 7 days)
[exit 3]

$ oops undo x
oops: "x" is not a snapshot id. oops list shows them; then run for example: oops undo 12
[exit 2]

$ oops show 7
oops: there is no snapshot 7. oops list shows the ones you have (they are kept 7 days)
[exit 3]

$ oops --yse
oops: oops does not have a --yse option. Did you mean --yes? Run oops --help to see its options.
[exit 2]

$ oops list extra
oops: oops list takes no arguments, but got "extra". Run oops list --help for how to use it.
[exit 2]

$ oops restore 1
oops: oops restore copies a snapshot into another folder, so it needs --to. Try: oops restore 1 --to ~/recovered (or oops undo 1 to restore in place)
[exit 2]

$ oops config keep_days
oops: oops config keep_days needs a value too. Try: oops config keep_days 14, or oops config with nothing to see every setting
[exit 2]

$ oops config nope 1
oops: "nope" is not a setting. The settings are keep_days, max_gb, free_floor_gb, quiet, store and watch_max_file_mb; oops config shows them all
[exit 2]

$ oops 
Nothing to undo. Oopsy has no unrestored snapshots.
Hooks are not installed yet. Run oops init to start protecting your commands.
[exit 0]

$ oops list
No snapshots yet.
Run oops init to install the hooks.
[exit 0]

$ oops db lsit
oops: oops db lsit is not a command. Did you mean oops db list? Run oops db --help to see them all.
[exit 2]

$ oops db list --url postgres://u:p@127.0.0.1:1/shop
oops: could not reach Postgres at 127.0.0.1:1: nothing is listening there. Start Postgres, or point Oopsy at the right server with --url or OOPS_DATABASE_URL
[exit 1]

$ oops db undo abc
oops: "abc" is not a change id. oops db list shows them; then run for example: oops db undo 42
[exit 2]

